// para.crdt (p2p P2 / P2.M) — the positive side of the `Mergeable` bound: every registered CRDT
// type satisfies `T: Mergeable`, so a generic `fn store<T: Mergeable>` accepts a gcounter,
// pncounter, and gset with a clean check (the bound is seeded from the CRDTs in the registry). The
// negative case — a plain value has no convergence story and is rejected E0025 — lives in
// `synced/mergeable_bound.noe`. (Was a `noeta-check` unit test over `std.crdt`; it moved here with
// the crdt module when it left `std` for `para`.)
// expect: stdout "ok"
// expect: exit 0
// `Mergeable` is imported because this program *names* it. It was a checker built-in, in scope
// everywhere without asking; as an ordinary native trait it follows the same rule as every other
// name — which is the point of the change, not a cost of it. A program that merely *calls*
// `synced_signal` still imports nothing: the bound there is the extension's own business.
use para.crdt.{Mergeable}
use para.{crdt}

fn store<T: Mergeable>(v: T): T { return v; }

g = store(crdt.gcounter())
p = store(crdt.pncounter())
s = store(crdt.gset())
echo "ok"
